00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Subtitles added by Hanson Hu

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Episode 20 - Quality Time

3
00:02:00,920 --> 00:02:02,380
Where's Mom?

4
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:04,560
She went to a school-board meeting.

5
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:06,840
I don't know how she does it.

6
00:02:06,840 --> 00:02:08,160
She sure keeps busy.

7
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:10,160
It's important to her.

8
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:13,260
There are lots of places to go,

9
00:02:13,400 --> 00:02:14,520
lots of things to do.

10
00:02:14,520 --> 00:02:17,860
She can't sit around and do nothing.

11
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:19,200
Philip works late.

12
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:21,100
I guess you're right.

13
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:23,900
I wish I had her energy.

14
00:02:24,580 --> 00:02:26,400
Anybody home?

15
00:02:26,460 --> 00:02:27,600
We're in here, Dad.

16
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:31,560
Oh. Hi, gang.

17
00:02:31,900 --> 00:02:32,800
Hello, Philip.

18
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:33,780
How was your day?

19
00:02:33,780 --> 00:02:36,380
My day was just fine.

20
00:02:36,840 --> 00:02:38,120
So was my night.

21
00:02:38,120 --> 00:02:40,080
It's almost ten o'clock.

22
00:02:40,080 --> 00:02:43,440
Mmm. I'm starving.

23
00:02:43,720 --> 00:02:44,560
Um... where's Mom?

24
00:02:44,680 --> 00:02:46,320
She went to a school-board meeting.

25
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:49,060
There's a note for you on the refrigerator.

26
00:02:49,520 --> 00:02:50,240
Oh?

27
00:03:26,880 --> 00:03:27,960
Did you have dinner, Robbie?

28
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:28,740
Yeah.

29
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:30,660
Mike and I had a hamburger at the diner.

30
00:03:30,660 --> 00:03:31,900
I came home a little while ago.

31
00:03:33,260 --> 00:03:34,560
You've been working late

32
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:36,020
almost every night this week, Dad.

33
00:03:36,020 --> 00:03:37,220
Aren't you exhausted?

34
00:03:38,580 --> 00:03:40,180
I don't have time to be exhausted.

35
00:03:41,060 --> 00:03:43,040
You and Mom haven't had dinner together with us

36
00:03:43,040 --> 00:03:44,360
in almost a full week.

37
00:03:44,860 --> 00:03:45,400
Yeah,

38
00:03:45,400 --> 00:03:47,120
I feel bad

39
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:48,640
about us not having dinner with the family,

40
00:03:48,640 --> 00:03:50,800
but our schedules are so different.

41
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:52,940
Either I'm at the hospital doing paperwork,

42
00:03:52,940 --> 00:03:54,840
or Mom is at a committee meeting.

43
00:03:55,320 --> 00:03:58,560
I frankly don't know what to do about it.

44
00:03:59,520 --> 00:04:01,240
I'm worried about you and Mom.

45
00:04:01,860 --> 00:04:04,000
You really have been working too hard.

46
00:04:04,820 --> 00:04:05,280
Well,

47
00:04:05,280 --> 00:04:06,600
I think I've had enough of that sandwich.

48
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:08,480
You didn't finish it.

49
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:14,400
It's not good to eat before going to bed.

50
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:18,160
A cookie can't hurt, though.

51
00:04:18,400 --> 00:04:18,800
Well,

52
00:04:19,940 --> 00:04:21,620
I'm heading off for bed

53
00:04:21,760 --> 00:04:23,440
and a good night's sleep.

54
00:04:23,940 --> 00:04:25,160
Well, good night.

55
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:26,360
Good night, Son.

56
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:29,760
Good night, Dad.

57
00:04:29,760 --> 00:04:30,460
I'm going to bed.

58
00:04:30,600 --> 00:04:31,560
Good night, Philip.

59
00:04:32,720 --> 00:04:34,520
Haven't you finished balancing that checkbook?

60
00:04:34,700 --> 00:04:36,800
I found another mistake.

61
00:04:37,660 --> 00:04:40,060
I'll be off to bed myself in a minute.

62
00:04:40,060 --> 00:04:41,600
OK. Good night.

63
00:04:50,040 --> 00:04:51,680
I'm really concerned about them,

64
00:04:51,680 --> 00:04:52,360
Grandpa.

65
00:04:52,720 --> 00:04:54,360
Concerned about whom?

66
00:04:54,520 --> 00:04:55,940
About Mom and Dad.

67
00:04:55,940 --> 00:04:58,260
They hardly ever see each other.

68
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:00,580
Dad often works late,

69
00:05:00,680 --> 00:05:02,660
and Mom has all these committees she's on.

70
00:05:02,900 --> 00:05:05,720
What do you propose to do about it?

71
00:05:05,840 --> 00:05:08,040
You have that look in your eye.

72
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:09,200
I don't know,

73
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:11,100
but there must be a way

74
00:05:11,100 --> 00:05:13,180
of getting them to spend more time together.

75
00:05:13,180 --> 00:05:14,700
Quality time.

76
00:05:24,080 --> 00:05:28,800
I got home late after working all day.

77
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:32,480
I was hoping to find you, but you were away.

78
00:05:32,680 --> 00:05:36,220
You left me some dinner, something to eat,

79
00:05:36,220 --> 00:05:41,120
but I won't be waiting up, 'cause I gotta get to sleep.

80
00:05:41,520 --> 00:05:45,700
I'm gonna get home late after working all day.

81
00:05:45,760 --> 00:05:49,460
I know you'll be home, but I'll miss you anyway.

82
00:05:49,480 --> 00:05:53,040
I hope you find my note and have something to eat.

83
00:05:53,040 --> 00:05:56,720
I really wanna see you, but you'll be asleep.

84
00:05:56,920 --> 00:06:02,480
We never seem to have any fun like we had before.

85
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:09,720
We're working too hard, and I never get to see you anymore.

86
00:06:11,840 --> 00:06:15,380
We have to make time for each other.

87
00:06:15,580 --> 00:06:19,120
We owe it to ourselves and one another.

88
00:06:19,320 --> 00:06:21,160
So why don't we do it?

89
00:06:21,360 --> 00:06:23,520
What do you say?

90
00:06:23,720 --> 00:06:24,980
Let's make some changes.

91
00:06:24,980 --> 00:06:26,800
We can start today.

92
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:31,440
We need some quality time together.

93
00:06:31,640 --> 00:06:35,280
What we need is quality time, oh yeah.

94
00:06:35,380 --> 00:06:38,000
We need to talk to each other,

95
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:40,320
laugh with each other.

96
00:06:40,520 --> 00:06:43,560
Let's get together, you and me,

97
00:06:43,760 --> 00:06:45,260
with quality time.

98
00:06:45,260 --> 00:06:48,480
We'll enjoy each other's company.

99
00:06:49,140 --> 00:06:53,120
You're working long hours, and you're never at home.

100
00:06:53,320 --> 00:06:56,400
Sometimes it seems you're just a voice on the phone.

101
00:06:56,400 --> 00:07:00,360
And when you call, you say you'll be home late.

102
00:07:00,560 --> 00:07:04,980
You're always missing dinner, 'cause it just can't wait.

103
00:07:05,840 --> 00:07:09,560
My job keeps me busy, and I know you're busy too.

104
00:07:09,620 --> 00:07:13,200
I really wanna spend more time with you.

105
00:07:13,340 --> 00:07:16,880
I'm getting up early, and I'm coming home late.

106
00:07:17,080 --> 00:07:21,500
If I wanna see you, I gotta make a special date.

107
00:07:21,700 --> 00:07:25,480
We need some quality time together.

108
00:07:25,480 --> 00:07:29,440
What we need is quality time, oh yeah.

109
00:07:29,640 --> 00:07:31,920
We need to talk to each other,

110
00:07:31,960 --> 00:07:34,000
laugh with each other.

111
00:08:59,040 --> 00:09:00,640
Hello there, Robbie.

112
00:09:01,440 --> 00:09:03,040
What are you doing up this late?

113
00:09:03,620 --> 00:09:04,320
Reading.

114
00:09:04,760 --> 00:09:05,700
Reading?

115
00:09:05,700 --> 00:09:06,840
At this hour?

116
00:09:07,120 --> 00:09:08,120
Ah, come on, Robbie.

117
00:09:08,120 --> 00:09:09,200
What are you doing up this late?

118
00:09:09,940 --> 00:09:11,340
Things on my mind.

119
00:09:14,320 --> 00:09:15,840
Do you care to talk about them?

120
00:09:17,560 --> 00:09:18,080
Sure,

121
00:09:18,080 --> 00:09:19,260
if you don't mind listening.

122
00:09:21,700 --> 00:09:23,120
Robbie, Robbie, remember me?

123
00:09:23,280 --> 00:09:24,240
I'm your mother.

124
00:09:24,360 --> 00:09:27,520
If you have something you want to talk about,

125
00:09:27,520 --> 00:09:29,280
I'm always prepared to listen.

126
00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:32,280
You haven't been around much lately.

127
00:09:34,460 --> 00:09:35,540
So that's it.

128
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:38,240
OK, let's talk.

129
00:09:38,560 --> 00:09:41,440
You and Dad are like ships that pass in the night.

130
00:09:41,720 --> 00:09:44,360
Dad works hard, and he works late.

131
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:46,880
You work hard on all your committees,

132
00:09:46,880 --> 00:09:48,020
and you work late.

133
00:09:48,240 --> 00:09:50,560
I thought you were proud of the work I do.

134
00:09:50,600 --> 00:09:51,800
I am, Mom.

135
00:09:51,800 --> 00:09:52,980
Real proud.

136
00:09:53,740 --> 00:09:56,160
You are one fantastic mom,

137
00:09:57,120 --> 00:10:00,200
but... but I've been noticing

138
00:10:00,200 --> 00:10:02,200
how little quality time

139
00:10:02,200 --> 00:10:05,480
you spend with Dad and me... and the family.

140
00:10:10,480 --> 00:10:12,080
It's a real problem, Robbie.

141
00:10:12,080 --> 00:10:14,000
I know it.

142
00:10:17,300 --> 00:10:18,740
I'm concerned.

143
00:10:19,620 --> 00:10:21,100
There must be a way

144
00:10:21,100 --> 00:10:23,520
that Dad and you can spend more time together.

145
00:10:25,700 --> 00:10:26,920
Well, we always talk about

146
00:10:26,920 --> 00:10:30,160
taking a vacation together with the family.

147
00:10:31,200 --> 00:10:32,360
I think you ought to

148
00:10:32,360 --> 00:10:35,480
take a vacation away from the family, alone.

149
00:10:35,720 --> 00:10:37,480
Kind of a second honeymoon.

150
00:10:38,920 --> 00:10:40,000
It would be wonderful,

151
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,080
but our schedules won't allow it.

152
00:10:42,240 --> 00:10:44,240
I think I have an idea.

153
00:10:45,980 --> 00:10:46,700
You do?

154
00:10:46,700 --> 00:10:47,400
Yup.

155
00:10:47,640 --> 00:10:49,240
I think I have an idea

156
00:10:49,320 --> 00:10:50,800
that will bring Dad and you

157
00:10:50,800 --> 00:10:52,640
together in a more scheduled way.

158
00:10:54,040 --> 00:10:54,880
What is it?

159
00:10:55,720 --> 00:10:56,120
Well,

160
00:10:56,120 --> 00:10:57,920
you know how Dad is always talking about

161
00:10:57,920 --> 00:10:58,840
the kids in the ward

162
00:10:58,840 --> 00:11:02,420
and how important it is for them to be paid attention to?

163
00:11:02,420 --> 00:11:02,820
Yes.

164
00:11:03,320 --> 00:11:03,720
Well...

165
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:04,600
and how hard it is

166
00:11:04,600 --> 00:11:06,940
because the doctors and nurses are so busy?

167
00:11:07,040 --> 00:11:07,700
Yes.

168
00:11:09,680 --> 00:11:11,960
Well, how would it be if you took some time

169
00:11:11,960 --> 00:11:14,760
to work with Dad towards solving that problem?

170
00:11:15,040 --> 00:11:16,640
I don't get it.

171
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:20,720
Like setting up a regular weekly reading program.

172
00:11:20,740 --> 00:11:22,280
You and Dad.

173
00:11:22,560 --> 00:11:24,480
You and Dr. Philip Stewart—

174
00:11:24,480 --> 00:11:27,320
going to the children's ward once or twice a week

175
00:11:27,380 --> 00:11:28,760
and reading to them.

176
00:11:29,760 --> 00:11:31,320
Not bad.

177
00:11:31,920 --> 00:11:33,920
Not a bad idea, Robbie.

178
00:11:34,040 --> 00:11:35,440
As a matter of fact,

179
00:11:35,440 --> 00:11:37,920
it fits right in with something I'm working on right now

180
00:11:37,920 --> 00:11:38,920
with the school-board committee.

181
00:11:39,120 --> 00:11:40,000
What's that?

182
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:42,200
I've been trying

183
00:11:42,200 --> 00:11:43,880
to work out a program

184
00:11:43,980 --> 00:11:45,180
in the public school

185
00:11:45,180 --> 00:11:49,300
that will bring parents and teachers together once a week

186
00:11:49,300 --> 00:11:52,320
to read to the students - their own children, really.

187
00:11:52,400 --> 00:11:54,920
By doing that, it will encourage reading.

188
00:11:55,440 --> 00:11:57,540
So it might fit in with a program

189
00:11:57,540 --> 00:11:59,600
for reading to the kids in the hospital.

190
00:12:00,380 --> 00:12:01,280
You're right.

191
00:12:01,680 --> 00:12:02,640
We'll do it!

192
00:12:02,720 --> 00:12:04,940
I'm going to talk to Daddy about it right now.

193
00:12:05,580 --> 00:12:08,480
But Dad was so exhausted when he came home from work.

194
00:12:08,820 --> 00:12:11,040
Why don't you talk to him about it tomorrow?

195
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,720
You are a very smart young man, Robbie.

196
00:12:14,760 --> 00:12:17,160
I think I'll wait until tomorrow.

197
00:12:18,240 --> 00:12:19,560
You won't forget, will you?

198
00:12:19,920 --> 00:12:21,640
Believe me, I won't.

199
00:12:21,920 --> 00:12:23,520
It is a great idea,

200
00:12:23,660 --> 00:12:25,700
and I promise you I won't forget.

201
00:12:26,260 --> 00:12:27,300
Thanks, Mom.

202
00:12:27,660 --> 00:12:28,940
Thank you, Robbie.

203
00:12:39,760 --> 00:12:41,360
I don't get it.

204
00:12:41,360 --> 00:12:43,960
I don't understand.

205
00:12:43,960 --> 00:12:45,240
I don't follow you.

206
00:12:45,240 --> 00:12:48,200
No, no. Oh, no, no.

207
00:12:48,400 --> 00:12:50,080
Run that by me again,

208
00:12:50,280 --> 00:12:51,640
'cause I don't understand.

209
00:12:51,640 --> 00:12:52,400
No, no.

210
00:12:52,600 --> 00:12:55,320
I don't get it.

211
00:12:56,120 --> 00:12:58,040
I don't get it.

212
00:12:58,260 --> 00:12:59,680
What do you mean?

213
00:12:59,780 --> 00:13:01,400
I don't get the picture.

214
00:13:01,400 --> 00:13:02,320
Huh?

215
00:13:02,380 --> 00:13:03,840
I don't get it.

216
00:13:03,840 --> 00:13:06,080
Explain it all again,

217
00:13:06,160 --> 00:13:07,240
'cause I don't understand.

218
00:13:07,440 --> 00:13:08,520
No, no.

219
00:13:08,720 --> 00:13:10,680
I don't get it.

220
00:13:10,880 --> 00:13:13,800
What are you trying to say?

221
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:15,820
What are you trying to say?

222
00:13:15,920 --> 00:13:17,760
I don't get it.

223
00:13:17,960 --> 00:13:20,520
Exactly what do you mean?

224
00:13:21,340 --> 00:13:23,820
I don't follow you.

225
00:13:24,660 --> 00:13:26,800
Could you explain that again?

226
00:13:28,200 --> 00:13:31,140
I don't get it.

227
00:13:31,340 --> 00:13:32,840
I don't understand.

228
00:13:32,840 --> 00:13:34,440
I don't follow you.

229
00:13:34,440 --> 00:13:36,520
No, no. Oh, no, no.

230
00:13:36,720 --> 00:13:38,900
Run that by me again,

231
00:13:38,900 --> 00:13:40,300
because I don't understand.

232
00:13:40,500 --> 00:13:41,340
No, no.

233
00:13:41,540 --> 00:13:44,200
I don't get it.

234
00:13:44,560 --> 00:13:47,160
Ah, I get it.

235
00:13:47,360 --> 00:13:50,660
Oh, I see what you mean.

236
00:13:50,860 --> 00:13:54,000
Yeah, I see what you're trying to say.

237
00:13:54,200 --> 00:13:56,940
I get it.

238
00:13:57,140 --> 00:13:59,800
Right, I see.

239
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:02,900
Oh, I know what you mean.

240
00:14:03,100 --> 00:14:05,380
OK, I follow you now.

241
00:14:05,380 --> 00:14:06,620
I understand.

242
00:14:06,800 --> 00:14:09,780
I get it.

243
00:14:11,540 --> 00:14:13,500
I get it.

244
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:14,280
Good morning.

245
00:15:15,280 --> 00:15:17,280
What a wonderful morning!

246
00:15:17,280 --> 00:15:19,200
Don't the flowers smell wonderful?

247
00:15:19,280 --> 00:15:20,760
Good morning, Ellen.

248
00:15:20,980 --> 00:15:22,960
Yes, they do.

249
00:15:23,200 --> 00:15:26,600
That's why I'm reading my paper and having my coffee

250
00:15:26,600 --> 00:15:28,000
on the patio this morning.

251
00:15:30,720 --> 00:15:32,280
Ah, it does smell sweet.

252
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,520
How was your school-board meeting last night?

253
00:15:36,520 --> 00:15:37,620
You must've come home very late.

254
00:15:37,880 --> 00:15:40,040
Did you find the sandwich I made for you?

255
00:15:40,140 --> 00:15:41,640
Thanks, dear.

256
00:15:44,440 --> 00:15:45,280
I was so tired

257
00:15:45,280 --> 00:15:46,280
I didn't even finish it.

258
00:15:48,360 --> 00:15:52,760
Philip, I've been working on this special project

259
00:15:52,760 --> 00:15:54,000
with the school board,

260
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,200
and I'd like your opinion about it.

261
00:15:56,440 --> 00:15:57,920
What is it?

262
00:15:59,480 --> 00:16:00,740
I've been trying to find

263
00:16:00,740 --> 00:16:03,280
a way to encourage reading.

264
00:16:04,480 --> 00:16:05,360
Good luck!

265
00:16:06,140 --> 00:16:08,440
Well, I think I may have found a way to do it.

266
00:16:08,800 --> 00:16:10,040
Tell me about it.

267
00:16:10,040 --> 00:16:12,360
I work with families every day, Ellen.

268
00:16:12,620 --> 00:16:15,420
I see how people spend their leisure time,

269
00:16:15,420 --> 00:16:16,360
young and old.

270
00:16:16,800 --> 00:16:18,660
Mostly watching television.

271
00:16:18,840 --> 00:16:20,880
Well, that would be OK

272
00:16:20,880 --> 00:16:22,180
if, and I repeat,

273
00:16:22,180 --> 00:16:25,620
if people took the time to read.

274
00:16:25,720 --> 00:16:27,180
I couldn't agree with you more.

275
00:16:27,180 --> 00:16:29,280
The question is,

276
00:16:29,480 --> 00:16:31,540
how do we get them to read more?

277
00:16:33,800 --> 00:16:34,240
I think

278
00:16:34,240 --> 00:16:35,960
you're going to give me the answer to that question.

279
00:16:35,960 --> 00:16:38,980
You have that look in your eye.

280
00:16:40,560 --> 00:16:41,840
I do have an answer, Philip.

281
00:16:41,840 --> 00:16:43,320
Or at least I think I do.

282
00:16:45,400 --> 00:16:46,360
Well, tell me about it.

283
00:16:47,920 --> 00:16:49,280
The plan is a simple one.

284
00:16:49,900 --> 00:16:53,840
Involve the entire family in a reading project.

285
00:16:54,660 --> 00:16:56,000
In the home?

286
00:16:56,360 --> 00:16:57,420
Yes, in the home.

287
00:16:57,420 --> 00:16:58,860
But first in the school-rooms.

288
00:17:01,560 --> 00:17:02,640
Hmm, interesting.

289
00:17:03,920 --> 00:17:05,760
But how do you plan to do that?

290
00:17:07,480 --> 00:17:09,420
By arranging with the public schools

291
00:17:09,440 --> 00:17:12,020
to schedule one hour a week to start with.

292
00:17:12,720 --> 00:17:13,780
During that time

293
00:17:13,780 --> 00:17:15,500
parents are invited to attend—

294
00:17:15,500 --> 00:17:18,240
and to read along with the children - their children.

295
00:17:20,080 --> 00:17:22,900
It can go beyond the school system, Ellen.

296
00:17:24,220 --> 00:17:25,000
Really?

297
00:17:26,620 --> 00:17:31,040
I guarantee you it would go very well in the hospitals.

298
00:17:31,340 --> 00:17:33,080
My patients, mostly kids,

299
00:17:33,080 --> 00:17:35,360
would love to read and be read to.

300
00:17:36,440 --> 00:17:37,160
You think so?

301
00:17:37,600 --> 00:17:38,940
I know so.

302
00:17:39,760 --> 00:17:41,960
Maybe we can experiment with your patients

303
00:17:42,520 --> 00:17:44,000
and see how the plan works.

304
00:17:45,320 --> 00:17:46,560
I love the idea.

305
00:17:48,920 --> 00:17:51,820
Would you work with me on it?

306
00:17:52,880 --> 00:17:56,260
I would love to, Philip.

307
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:00,920
And that way,

308
00:18:01,960 --> 00:18:03,880
we'll spend more time together, Ellen.

309
00:18:04,520 --> 00:18:07,440
We just don't see each other anymore.

310
00:18:08,780 --> 00:18:11,240
You and I are very busy these days.

311
00:18:11,240 --> 00:18:11,860
This is true.

312
00:18:12,600 --> 00:18:14,940
We need to find time to be together more,

313
00:18:14,940 --> 00:18:16,480
to do things together more, you and I.

314
00:18:17,840 --> 00:18:19,960
This would be a wonderful way to accomplish that.

315
00:18:20,080 --> 00:18:21,040
I have a question.

316
00:18:21,740 --> 00:18:22,520
Yes?

317
00:18:23,080 --> 00:18:24,320
What do we read?

318
00:18:25,920 --> 00:18:27,280
To the patients in the ward?

319
00:18:27,280 --> 00:18:28,020
Yes.

320
00:18:29,720 --> 00:18:30,560
Well,

321
00:18:30,700 --> 00:18:32,000
let's, you and I, talk about it.

322
00:18:32,800 --> 00:18:34,520
What would you like to read to them?

323
00:18:47,400 --> 00:18:49,080
Mrs. Stewart and I

324
00:18:49,080 --> 00:18:51,120
will read a poem by Robert Frost.

325
00:18:52,140 --> 00:18:52,680
It's called

326
00:18:52,680 --> 00:18:55,020
"Stopping by Woods on a Snowy Evening".

327
00:18:56,280 --> 00:18:57,720
Would you begin, Ellen?

328
00:18:58,040 --> 00:18:58,680
All right.

329
00:19:01,880 --> 00:19:04,460
"Stopping by Woods on a Snowy Evening"

330
00:19:05,520 --> 00:19:07,000
by Robert Frost.

331
00:19:08,320 --> 00:19:10,700
Whose woods these are I think I know.

332
00:19:11,160 --> 00:19:12,780
His house is in the village though;

333
00:19:12,780 --> 00:19:16,100
He will not see me stopping here

334
00:19:16,100 --> 00:19:19,000
To watch his woods fill up with snow.

335
00:19:19,480 --> 00:19:21,760
My little horse must think it queer

336
00:19:21,800 --> 00:19:24,200
To stop without a farmhouse near

337
00:19:24,620 --> 00:19:27,120
Between the woods and frozen lake

338
00:19:27,120 --> 00:19:30,720
The darkest evening of the year.

339
00:19:30,960 --> 00:19:33,640
He gives his harness bells a shake

340
00:19:33,640 --> 00:19:35,320
To ask if there is some mistake.

341
00:19:35,440 --> 00:19:38,000
The only other sound's the sweep

342
00:19:38,320 --> 00:19:41,720
Of easy wind and downy flake.

343
00:19:42,600 --> 00:19:45,720
The woods are lovely, dark and deep,

344
00:19:46,260 --> 00:19:48,040
But I have promises to keep,

345
00:19:48,280 --> 00:19:51,200
And miles to go before I sleep,

346
00:19:51,380 --> 00:19:55,300
And miles to go before I sleep.

347
00:20:00,900 --> 00:20:04,360
You two belong on stage! That was wonderful!

348
00:20:04,860 --> 00:20:05,260
Grandpa!

349
00:20:05,480 --> 00:20:07,760
Dad... Robbie. When did you come?

350
00:20:08,220 --> 00:20:09,920
We've been listening to you both.

351
00:20:10,520 --> 00:20:12,520
These are lucky kids.

352
00:20:12,800 --> 00:20:15,160
Do you enjoy reading together?

353
00:20:15,500 --> 00:20:19,000
Well, we may read together aloud at home.

354
00:20:19,760 --> 00:20:21,340
You were right, Robbie.

355
00:20:22,560 --> 00:20:23,480
I know.

